This example links host.cpp and device.cu into the cpp_integration executable.
From the parent directory, configure the standalone CMake project as described
in README.md, then run:

  cmake --build build-cpu --target cpp_integration
  ./build-cpu/bin/cpp_integration

With THRUST_BACKEND=CUDA, device.cu is compiled by the CUDA compiler, and
host.cpp by the C++ compiler. With CPP, OMP, or TBB, both use the CPU backend;
a generated C++ wrapper makes device.cu usable by an ordinary C++ compiler.

Recent CCCL releases include CUDA architectures in their ABI namespace.
For CUDA builds, CMake generates wrappers that place Thrust and CUB in the
same private namespace in both translation units, avoiding a host/device
linkage mismatch while retaining separate C++ and CUDA compilation.
